The model, verified:

```js
// A component is a function: props in, UI description out
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;  // WHAT to render, not HOW
}

// Same input → same output (the declarative contract)
// <Greeting name="Ada" /> always renders <h1>Hello, Ada</h1>
```

The three ideas that make React React:
- Components: UI split into reusable functions — a page is a tree of components
- Declarative: you write `UI = f(state)` — when state changes, React re-renders and diffs
- Virtual DOM: React keeps a lightweight copy of the UI tree, diffs old-vs-new, and applies only the changed DOM operations

The declarative contrast (what interviewers want you to articulate): in vanilla JS you imperatively find elements and mutate them (`document.getElementById('x').textContent = 'new'`); in React you never touch the DOM — you change state and describe the result. React reconciles the two trees and updates the real DOM minimally.

Why teams adopt it (the practical answer): component reuse across pages/products, a huge ecosystem (React Native for mobile, Next.js for SSR), one mental model for everything from widgets to apps, and hiring pool familiarity.
